How recitations work
Most recitations begin with a 10-minute quiz. The rest of the session is spent working on the week’s worksheet, individually or in small groups.
The redesigned worksheets (from Week 7, October 12). Inspired by Professor Jinfa Cai’s talk in my mathematics education class, I redesigned the worksheets around problem posing: students change hypotheses, formulate their own questions, and exchange problems with classmates. Students keep their worksheets, which include definitions and notes they can study from, and hand in only a short exit ticket at the end of each recitation. Since making these changes, I have seen student participation increase, and students have responded positively to the new format.
A typical redesigned packet includes:

a warm-up that surfaces the key idea, such as sorting maps by whether they are injective or surjective;
proof frameworks: the first and last lines of a proof of “injective”, “surjective”, “not injective”, and so on;
investigations that turn computed examples into conjectures;
problem-posing tasks, where students change a hypothesis or write their own question and trade it with a classmate;
“grade the proof” tasks, where students find and repair common logical errors;
an exit ticket, the only part handed in, to check understanding at the end of class.
